((5-1)/|p+q|) + 1 = floor(4/|p+q|) + 1. For |p+q|=1, this gives floor(4/1)+1 = 5. But due to x constraints, we get at most floor((k+1)/2) = floor(6/2) = 3. So for |p+q|=1, the bound is 3. For |p+q|=2, floor(4/2)+1 = 2+1 = 3. So also 3. For |p+q|=3, floor(4/3)+1 = 1+1 = 2. So cannot get 3. So only lines with |p+q|=1 or 2 can have up to 3 points. So we need to consider lines with |p+q|=1 or 2.\n\nNow, we need to find all such lines that intersect T_5 in exactly 3 points. Let's enumerate possibilities for p+q = 1 (or -1) and p+q = 2 (or -2). For each, we need to find lines that intersect T_5 in exactly 3 points.\n\nCase 1: p+q = 1. Then p = 1 - q. Since p≠0, q≠1. So q≥2. p = 1 - q ≤ -1. So p negative. gcd(p,q)=1. So q and p are coprime. For q=2, p=-1. For q=3, p=-2 (gcd( -2,3)=1). For q=4, p=-3 (gcd( -3,4)=1). For q=5, p=-4 (gcd( -4,5)=1). So infinite possibilities.\n\nNow, the line equation: p x - q y = c. Let's find c such that the line passes through points in T_5. For each line, we can find the set of integer points in T_5. Let's try to find lines with exactly 3 points.\n\nWe can also think of lines of the form x + q y = d (since p = 1 - q, so p x - q y = (1 - q) x - q y = c => rearrange? Actually p x - q y = c => (1 - q) x - q y = c => -q x - q y + x = c => x - q (x + y) = c => x = c + q (x + y). That's messy. Let's instead use the parametric form: Since p+q = 1, the sum x+y changes by 1 each step. So the line will intersect each diagonal x+y = constant exactly once (if it intersects at all). So the line will intersect T_5 at points with sums from some minimal sum to some maximal sum, each sum giving at most one point. So the number of points equals the number of sums in the range [2,6] that the line hits. Since sum changes by 1 each step, the line can intersect up to 5 points (if it hits all sums). But due to x constraints, it may be limited to 3. So we need to find lines that intersect exactly 3 sums within [2,6] and also satisfy x,y≥1.\n\nLet's try to find lines with p+q=1 and q=2 (p=-1). The line equation: -1*x - 2*y = c => x + 2y = -c. So we can write x + 2y = d, where d is integer. The sum x+y = (d - y) + y = d - y + y = d? Wait x = d - 2y, so sum x+y = d - 2y + y = d - y. So sum = d - y. As y varies, sum changes by -1 each step (since y increments by 1, sum decreases by 1). So indeed sum changes by 1 each step. So the line intersects each diagonal x+y = constant exactly once. The possible y values must be positive integer, and x = d - 2y must be positive integer. So we need y≥1, x≥1 => d - 2y ≥ 1 => y ≤ (d-1)/2. Also y≥1 => y ≤ floor((d-1)/2). So the number of integer y satisfying 1 ≤ y ≤ floor((d-1)/2) is floor((d-1)/2). So the number of points on the line is floor((d-1)/2). But also we need sum x+y = d - y ≤ 6 (since sum ≤6). So d - y ≤ 6 => y ≥ d - 6. So y must satisfy max(1, d-6) ≤ y ≤ floor((d-1)/2). So the number of integer y in that interval is floor((d-1)/2) - max(1, d-6) + 1, if positive.\n\nWe need this number to be exactly 3. Let's find d values that give exactly 3 points in T_5.\n\nCompute for d from minimal possible to maximal. Since x≥1, y≥1, and sum ≤6, we have constraints: x+y = d - y ≤ 6 => d ≤ 6 + y. Since y≥1, d ≤ 7. Also x = d - 2y ≥ 1 => d ≥ 2y + 1. Since y≥1, d ≥ 3. So d ∈ [3,7]. Let's test each d:\n\n- d=3: y must satisfy max(1, 3-6= -3) = 1 ≤ y ≤ floor((3-1)/2) = floor(2/2) = 1. So y=1 only. Then x = 3 - 2*1 = 1. So point (1,1). Sum = 2. So 1 point.\n\n- d=4: y ∈ [max(1,4-6= -2)=1, floor((4-1)/2)= floor(3/2)=1]. So y=1 only. x = 4 - 2 = 2. So (2,1). Sum = 3. So 1 point.\n\n- d=5: y ∈ [max(1,5-6=-1)=1, floor((5-1)/2)= floor(4/2)=2]. So y=1,2. For y=1: x=5-2=3 => (3,1) sum=4. For y=2: x=5-4=1 => (1,2) sum=3. So 2 points.\n\n- d=6: y ∈ [max(1,6-6=0)=1, floor((6-1)/2)= floor(5/2)=2]. So y=1,2. y=1: x=6-2=4 => (4,1) sum=5. y=2: x=6-4=2 => (2,2) sum=4. So 2 points.\n\n- d=7: y ∈ [max(1,7-6=1)=1, floor((7-1)/2)= floor(6/2)=3]. So y=1,2,3. y=1: x=7-2=5 => (5,1) sum=6. y=2: x=7-4=3 => (3,2) sum=5. y=3: x=7-6=1 => (1,3) sum=4. So 3 points. So line x+2y=7 has exactly 3 points in T_5. So this line is a candidate.\n\nThus lines with p+q=1 (q=2) can achieve the bound. Similarly, lines with p+q=-1 (p = -1 - q) also can achieve bound.\n\nNow, case 2: p+q = 2. Then p = 2 - q. Since p≠0, q≠2. So q can be 1 (p=1) => p+q=2. q=3 => p=-1 => p+q=2. q=4 => p=-2 => p+q=2. q=5 => p=-3 => p+q=2. So infinite possibilities. For q=1, p=1 => line x - y = c => slope 1. For q=3, p=-1 => line -x - 3y = c => x + 3y = -c => slope -1/3. For q=5, p=-3 => line -3x - 5y = c => x + (5/3) y = -c/3? Actually slope p/q = -3/5 = -0.6. So many possibilities.\n\nNow, we need to find lines with exactly 3 points in T_5. Let's test p=1,q=1 (slope 1). Equation: x - y = c => y = x - c. Points in T_5: x≥1, y≥1 => x - c ≥ 1 => x ≥ c+1. Also sum x+y = x + (x - c) = 2x - c ≤ 6 => x ≤ (6 + c)/2. Also x+y ≥ 2 => 2x - c ≥ 2 => x ≥ (2 + c)/2. So x must be integer satisfying max(c+1, ceil((2 + c)/2)) ≤ x ≤ floor((6 + c)/2). Let's find c values that give exactly 3 integer x.\n\nWe can also think of line y = x - c. For each integer c, the line passes through points (x, x - c). The sum is 2x - c. So as x increments by 1, sum increments by 2. So the sum changes by 2 each step. So the number of points in T_5 is at most floor((6 - 2)/2) + 1 = floor(4/2)+1 = 2+1 = 3. So lines with slope 1 can achieve up to 3 points. Indeed, for c=0 (line y = x), points: (1,1) sum=2; (2,2) sum=4; (3,3) sum=6; (4,4) sum=8 >6, so only three points: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). So line y=x has exactly 3 points in T_5. So slope 1 line works.\n\nNow, lines with p+q = -2 (i.e., p+q = -2) also have bound 3. For p+q = -2, we have p = -2 - q. For q=1, p=-3 => slope -3. For q=2, p=-4 => slope -2. For q=3, p=-5 => slope -5/3, etc. But we need to check if any of those lines can have exactly 3 points. For example, line with p=-2,q=1 (p+q=-1? Wait p=-2,q=1 => p+q=-1, not -2). So p+q = -2 would be p=-3,q=1 (p+q=-2). That line is -3x - y = c => 3x + y = -c => slope