hoices.\n\nLet's see if we can find a different partition. Perhaps we can use a line with slope -1/2 but different constant to cover other points. Or use a line with p+q=1 but different q.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use a line with slope -1/3 (p=-1,q=3) which yields x + 3y = d. Let's see if we can cover some points.\n\nBut before exploring further, note that we need to partition 15 points into 5 lines of 3 points each. The lines must be distinct. The lines we have used: L1 (y=x), L2 (x+2y=7), L3 (y=-2x+7). That's 3 lines. We need 2 more lines covering the remaining 6 points. Let's list remaining points after L1, L2, L3:\n\nOriginal set: all points.\n\nL1: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\nL2: (5,1),(3,2),(1,3)\nL3: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\n\nNow remove these from the full set:\n\nFull set:\n(1,1) used\n(1,2) not used\n(2,1) not used\n(1,3) used\n(2,2) used\n(3,1) used\n(1,4) not used\n(2,3) used\n(3,2) used\n(4,1) not used\n(1,5) used\n(2,4) not used\n(3,3) used\n(4,2) not used\n(5,1) used\n\nRemaining points: (1,2),(2,1),(1,4),(4,1),(2,4),(4,2). That's 6 points. Good, we need 2 lines each covering 3 points.\n\nNow we need to find two sunny lines each covering exactly 3 of these points, and they must be disjoint (no overlapping points). Let's see if we can find a line covering (1,2),(2,1),(? ) maybe (3,0) invalid. So line through (1,2) and (2,1) has slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1. That's slope -1, which is forbidden (parallel to line x+y=0). So cannot use that line. So we cannot pair (1,2) and (2,1) on a sunny line. So we need to find lines that cover three points each, but not using slope -1.\n\nLet's examine possible lines through (1,2) and (1,4). That's vertical line x=1, which is non-sunny (parallel to y-axis). So not allowed. So we cannot have a sunny line covering (1,2) and (1,4). So (1,2) must be paired with points that are not same x or same y, and not slope -1.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (4,1). Slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3. That's allowed (not -1, not 0, not infinite). So lin