floor(k/q) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2).\" This argument only applies to p+q = 1 case, where q ≥ 2. For p+q = -1 case, they didn't discuss the refined bound. But we need to check if p+q = -1 lines can achieve the bound floor((k+1)/2). Let's examine p+q = -1, q = 1, p = -2 (slope -2). For T_k, the parametric representation: (x0 + t, y0 -2t). Sum x+y = (x0+y0) - t. So sum changes by -1 each step. The number of points in T_k is at most floor((k-1)/1) + 1 = k. But we need to bound it by floor((k+1)/2). However, they claim that for p+q = -1, the bound is also floor((k+1)/2) because of the x coordinate constraint: x = x0 + t must stay in [1,k]. So t must be such that x0 + t ∈ [1,k]. So the number of t values is at most floor((k - 1)/|q|) + 1? Actually we need to consider both constraints: sum constraint and x constraint. Let's derive the maximum number of points a line with p+q = -1 can have in T_k.\n\nGeneral line: p x - q y = c, with p+q = -1. So p = -1 - q. Since gcd(p,q)=1, q can be any integer >0 such that p and q are coprime. For q = 1, p = -2. For q = 2, p = -3. For q = 3, p = -4, etc. So slopes are -2, -3/2, -4/3, etc. The parametric representation: (x0 + q t, y0 + p t) = (x0 + q t, y0 + (-1 - q) t) = (x0 + q t, y0 - t - q t) = (x0 + q t, y0 - t - q t). So sum x+y = (x0 + y0) - t. So sum decreases by 1 each step. So the sum constraint yields at most k possible points (since sum must be between 2 and k+1). However, the x coordinate constraint: x = x0 + q t must be between 1 and k. So t must satisfy 1 ≤ x0 + q t ≤ k. So t ∈ [ (1 - x0)/q, (k - x0)/q ]. The length of this interval is at most (k - 1)/q (plus maybe 1). So number of integer t values ≤ floor((k - 1)/q) + 1. So the number of points is bounded by min(k, floor((k - 1)/q) + 1). Since q ≥ 1, floor((k - 1)/q) + 1 ≤ k (with equality only if q = 1). So for q = 1, the bound is floor((k - 1)/1) + 1 = k. So a line with p+q = -1 and q = 1 can have up to k points in T_k. But we need to check if such a line can actually have k